      "text": "The parametric equations for the given curve are:\n\n\\[\nx = 2 \\sin t, \\quad y = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the length of the curve from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\). The formula for the arc length \\( L \\) of a parametric curve \\((x(t), y(t))\\) from \\( t = a \\) to \\( t = b \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFirst, we find the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{\\left( 2 \\cos t \\right)^2 + \\left( -2 \\sin t \\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFactor out the 4:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nUse the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4} \\, dt\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nEvaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_0^\\pi dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_0^\\pi\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\) is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
