      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The length of a parametric curve given by $(x(t), y(t))$ from $t = a$ to $t = b$ is given by the integral:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\nFor the given parametric curve $(x(t), y(t)) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)$ from $t = 0$ to $t = \\pi$, we first find the derivatives:\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\nNow, we substitute these derivatives into the length formula:\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2 \\pi\n\\]\nThus, the length of the parametric curve from $t = 0$ to $t = \\pi$ is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable t\nt = sp.symbols('t')\n\n# Define the parametric equations\nx = 2 * sp.sin(t)\ny = 2 * sp.cos(t)\n\n# Calculate the derivatives\ndx_dt = sp.diff(x, t)\ndy_dt = sp.diff(y, t)\n\n# Define the integrand for the length of the curve\nintegrand = sp.sqrt(dx_dt**2 + dy_dt**2)\n\n# Compute the definite integral from 0 to π\nlength = sp.integrate(integrand, (t, 0, sp.pi))\nprint(length)\n```",
